odd-extensions.exceptional-algebra-resid.formal:
For the tame wall of Proposition \claimref{odd-extensions.line-tame-wall}:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] the sheaf paper's rescaling of the dying direction, $w_{\Lambda} \mapsto w_{\Lambda}/\sqrt{|\Lambda|}$, is proportional, by the fixed factor $\sqrt 3$, to the dark-energy paper's normalisation of the Stelle-West vector to unit length, $e_{V} = V/|V|$, both being normalisations of the same line; the exceptional algebra at the wall is the Lorentz frame orthogonal to the rate of degeneration, $\Cl(1,3) \perp \langle +\tfrac13\rangle \cong \Cl(2,3)$ approached from $\Lambda < 0$ and $\Cl(1,3) \perp \langle -\tfrac13\rangle \cong \Cl(1,4)$ from $\Lambda > 0$, the cross terms vanishing identically so that the rescaled family is exactly, not asymptotically, block diagonal;
\item[(ii)] the residue of the inverse form at the wall is $-3\,w_{0} \otimes w_{0}$, the coefficient fixed by the normalisation $Q(w_{\Lambda}) = -\Lambda/3$: the pole is the Stelle-West norm, $\Lambda\,Q(V) = -3$, one algebraic step from the imported identity of Fact \claimref{odd-extensions.imported-frozen-point-degenerate-algebras}; the sheaf paper's identification $N^{*}\Sigma \cong L^{\otimes 2}$ reads $d\Lambda \cong -3\, w_{0} \otimes w_{0}$, which is $-3\,I_{0} \otimes I_{0}$ when $a(0)^{2} = 1$, so the dying direction carries the weight of $|\Lambda|^{1/2}$ and the weightless normalisation of the frozen point's complement is by $\sqrt{|\Lambda|/3}$, which for $\Lambda > 0$ is the asymptotic de Sitter rate $H = \sqrt{\Lambda/3}$, the series' $H^{2} = Q(I)$;
\item[(iii)] the rung, with its tractor metric of constant signature $(2,4)$, is a nondegenerate completion of the family on both sides of the wall, of rank one more, the completing direction at the wall being the partner $\varphi$ and away from it the frozen point $I_{\Lambda}$ itself; this is not particular to the cosmological constant: for any $(p,q)$ the family of hyperplanes $\mathrm{span}(f_{1}, \ldots, f_{n}, u + \tfrac{t}{2}v)$ of $\R^{p,q} \perp H$, $H$ hyperbolic with null basis $u, v$, $\ip{u}{v} = 1$, is a tame corank-one family with dying eigenvalue $t$ completed on both sides by the fixed $(p+1,q+1)$, and the $\Lambda$ family is this model with $(p,q) = (1,3)$, $u = I_{0}$, $v = \varphi$, $t = -\Lambda/3$;
\item[(iv)] this does not contradict the conservation of degeneracy, and lies in the case its proof excludes by hypothesis: the complement of $I_{\Lambda}^{\perp}$ in the rung is $\mathrm{span}(I_{\Lambda})$ with $K = Q(I_{\Lambda}) = \Lambda/3$, whose signature is $(1,0)$ on one side and $(0,1)$ on the other, so the completion is not standard in the sense of Fact \claimref{odd-extensions.imported-tame-walls-exceptional-algebra-residue}, and the totals $(2,3) + (0,1) = (1,4) + (1,0) = (2,4)$ match because the complement's line changes type across the wall; on the $\Lambda$ line, where the base is contractible and the wall a point, the conservation theorem reduces to the local constancy of a signature and its content is the constancy clause on $K$ alone.
\end{enumerate}

odd-extensions.line-tame-wall.formal:
Fix the dilation gauge by a smooth nowhere-vanishing $a(\Lambda)$ and write the frozen point in the canonical null frame as $I_{\Lambda} = a I_{0} + b\varphi$ with $2ab = \Lambda/3$ (Theorem \claimref{odd-extensions.transport-constrains-frozen-point-only-through}). Let $w_{\Lambda} = aI_{0} - b\varphi$, so that $I_{\Lambda}^{\perp}$ is spanned by the Lorentz frame and $w_{\Lambda}$. Then the family $(I_{\Lambda}^{\perp}, \text{induced form})$ over the $\Lambda$ line is a tame corank-one degeneration in the sense of Fact \claimref{odd-extensions.imported-tame-walls-exceptional-algebra-residue}:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] its Gram matrix in the frame $(e_{1}, \ldots, e_{4}, w_{\Lambda})$ is $\operatorname{diag}(1,-1,-1,-1,-\Lambda/3)$ for every $\Lambda$ and every gauge, so the degenerate locus is the point $\Lambda = 0$ (T1), the radical there is the line spanned by $I_{0}$ (T2), and the dying eigenvalue $-\Lambda/3$ crosses zero with nonzero speed (T3), $\delta(w_{0} \otimes w_{0}) = -\tfrac13\,d\Lambda$ in the parametrisation by $\Lambda$, the $3$ being that of $Q(I) = \Lambda/3$;
\item[(ii)] the signatures on the two sides are $(2,3)$ for $\Lambda < 0$ and $(1,4)$ for $\Lambda > 0$: the wall separates the anti-de Sitter and de Sitter intermediate algebras, and crossing it changes the sign of one direction's square, which is the sheaf paper's signature-change wall in a parameter rather than in spacetime;
\item[(iii)] the gauge $a = b$, which makes $I_{\Lambda}$ proportional to $I_{0} + \varphi$, is not smooth at the wall and is not defined for $\Lambda < 0$; the tame structure needs a smooth gauge, and the scalar invariants (the dying eigenvalue, its crossing speed, the signatures) do not depend on which, while the identification of the radical vector with $I_{0}$ at a fixed normalisation does, $w_{0} = a(0)\,I_{0}$.
\end{enumerate}

sheaf.differential-closure-filtration-co.formal:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $\mathcal{A}^{\log}$ is a commutative algebra containing $\mathcal{A}$, stable under the full boundary calculus: under $\tau\partial_\tau$ and under all tangential vector fields. The differential closure of $\mathcal{A}$ therefore lies inside $\mathcal{A}^{\log}$, and by Lemma \claimref{sheaf.log-obstruction} it exceeds $\mathcal{A}$ as soon as one exponent is nonconstant.
\item The logarithms are filtered by tangential differentiation: $N$ tangential derivatives applied to $\mathcal{A}$ produce log degree at most $N$, with top log coefficient $(e')^N$ on a monomial $\tau^{e}$; radial derivatives do not raise the log degree.
\item No operation of the calculus lowers the exponent set. Consequently every element of $\mathcal{A}^{\log}$ is conormal: for b-vector fields $V_1,\dots,V_k$ and every $\varepsilon>0$, $V_1\cdots V_k a = O(\tau^{\,e_{\min}(x)-\varepsilon})$ locally uniformly.
\item The log-free part with locally constant exponents is precisely the smooth-coefficient polyhomogeneous ring of \cite{Melrose1993}, index sets generated by the exponents; $\mathcal{A}$ itself is the log-degree-zero part of $\mathcal{A}^{\log}$: a subalgebra, but not a submodule over the calculus unless the exponents are locally constant.
\end{enumerate}

sheaf.function-linearity-tame-constructi.formal:
\leavevmode
\begin{enumerate}
\item Every construction of this paper, the degeneration sheaf and its ideal (Theorem \claimref{sheaf.degeneration-sheaf}), the coupling and the ambient form (Definition \claimref{obj.sheaf.collar-data-coupling}), the one-sided completion (Theorem \claimref{sheaf.one-sided-completion}), the ladder pair (Corollary \claimref{sheaf.localised-transport}), the exceptional rescaling (Proposition \claimref{sheaf.exceptional-algebra-deformation-normal-cone}) and the weighted module (Definition \claimref{obj.sheaf.weighted-coframe-module}), is $C^\infty$-linear over the base: it is obtained from the input data by operations that are linear over the function algebra, and it never differentiates a coefficient function.
\item The class of $C^\infty$-linear operations is closed under composition, under sums and multiplication by functions, under the tensor operations and Clifford multiplication, and under passage to the $C^\infty$-submodules and quotients the constructions form (the ideal, the quotient algebra, the even part, the weighted module). Every composite of the constructions therefore remains in the class; the class-membership of an assembled object never has to be re-argued.
\item Consequently every endomorphism \emph{supplied by} the constructions, of any bundle they build, is $C^\infty$-linear, hence tensorial: a bundle map, and on a line bundle multiplication by a function. The quantifier is over the supplied endomorphisms, not over all endomorphisms of the supplied bundles: an arbitrary endomorphism of a bundle the constructions build, a differential operator on its sections, say, need not be $C^\infty$-linear, and none such is supplied.
\end{enumerate}

sheaf.invariant-form-t3.formal:
Assume (T1) and (T2). Extend a local spanning section $k$ of $\shL$ to a covector field on a neighbourhood of $\Sig$ in $M$, and let $g_k := G(k,k)$, a smooth function vanishing on $\Sig$. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $dg_k|_\Sig$ annihilates $T\Sig$, hence is a section of the conormal bundle $N^*\Sig$;
\item $dg_k|_\Sig$ is independent of the chosen extension of $k$;
\item under $k \mapsto ck$ ($c$ a nonvanishing function), $dg_k|_\Sig$ scales by $c^2|_\Sig$.
\end{enumerate}
Consequently the assignment $k \otimes k \mapsto dg_k|_\Sig$ is a well-defined bundle map
\[
\delta \;:\; \shL \otimes \shL \;\longrightarrow\; N^*\Sig
\]
over $\Sig$, canonically attached to the degeneration. Both sides are line bundles, so $\delta$ is nowhere zero if and only if it is an isomorphism $\shL^{\otimes 2} \cong N^*\Sig$; hypothesis (T3) is exactly the demand that the degeneration itself identify the square of the dying line with the conormal line.

sheaf.rain-duality.formal:
At every interior radius, exactly:
\begin{enumerate}
\item the coframe $(\dtPG,\ k/\sqrt r,\ \mu_1,\ \mu_2)$ is the \emph{dual coframe} of the infalling orthonormal frame: the pairing matrix is the identity, the vanishing $(k/\sqrt r)(V) = 0$ being the rain identity of Theorem \claimref{sheaf.schwarzschild-degeneration-boundary-tame}(5);
\item its Gram is exactly Minkowski, $\operatorname{diag}(1,-1,-1,-1)$, at every radius, horizon and collar included: $\dtPG$, the Painlev\'e-Gullstrand time, is the rain's proper-time differential, and the rain observer's covector space never degenerates;
\item a $u$-smooth one-form lies in $\shW$ if and only if its rain-frame components are bounded up to the singularity; the norm identity $|\alpha|_G^2 = \alpha(V)^2 - \sum_i \alpha(e_{\hat\imath})^2$ holds exactly, though boundedness of the norm alone does not suffice for membership (Remark \claimref{note.sheaf.bounded-norm-alone-does-not-characterise}); the excluded coordinate forms diverge, $dr(V) = -m/\sqrt r$ and $d\theta(e_{\hat\theta}) = 1/r$;
\item the module $\shW_{\mathrm{exc}}$ spanned by the coframe of (1) over $C^\infty(\Mt_u)$ is the exceptional-regime module of Proposition \claimref{sheaf.exceptional-algebra-black-hole}; rain-frame components of a covector are precisely its $\shW_{\mathrm{exc}}$-coefficients; and $\shW \subset \shW_{\mathrm{exc}}$, via $\rho = \sqrt r\,(k/\sqrt r) - m\,\dtPG$, with transition determinant $-\sqrt r$. The weighted module is the $u$-smooth shadow of the rain observer's covector space, and the degeneration-regime/exceptional-regime dichotomy of Theorem \claimref{sheaf.weights-are-forced-are-kasner-exponents} is the dichotomy between the blow-up's smooth structure and the rain frame.
\end{enumerate}

sheaf.tame-wall-induction.formal:
Assume $\Sig$ compact, and fix the collar. Near the wall, on the far side, $s$ is strictly monotone along transversals: $s = 1/\lambda + O(1)$ with derivative $s' = -\lambda'/\lambda^2\,(1 + o(1)) \neq 0$, since the singular term dominates the bounded derivative of the smooth remainder; call the (relatively compact) region where this monotonicity holds the \emph{monotone zone}, and let
\[
S \;:=\; \sup\,|s| \quad\text{over the far-side part of the collar \emph{outside} the monotone zone}
\]
(finite: that region has compact closure avoiding $\Sig$). Call the coupling \emph{good} if $|\mu\nu| < 1/(2S)$. For good couplings:
\begin{enumerate}
\item every far-side zero of $F$ lies in the monotone zone with $\beta \equiv 1$ there, so the shadow wall $\Sig'$ is the regular level set $\{F = 0\}$ with $dF = 2\mu\nu\,ds \neq 0$ transversally: a smooth closed hypersurface, and the zero of $\det h$ across it is simple;
\item at each point of $\Sig'$ exactly one $h$-eigenvalue (with respect to an auxiliary frame metric) vanishes, to first order, and the radical line of $h|_{\Sig'}$ is a smooth line subbundle: it is the column space of the adjugate $\operatorname{adj}(h)$, which has rank one along $\Sig'$;
\item the invariant transversality datum of Lemma \claimref{sheaf.invariant-form-t3} (which makes sense verbatim for any bundle with symmetric form) is nowhere zero on $\Sig'$: by Jacobi's formula the simplicity of the zero of $\det h$ \emph{is} the transversality of the degeneration.
\end{enumerate}
Hence $(T, h)$ is itself a tame corank-one degeneration along $\Sig'$, in the bundle-valued sense of Remark \claimref{note.sheaf.setup-generalises-arbitrary-bundles}. The completion therefore iterates: each wall's ambient form is a tame degeneration with its own shadow wall, and the infinite wall sequence exists unconditionally for good couplings, not merely generically.

sheaf.tameness.formal:
The pair $(M, G)$ is a \emph{tame corank-one degeneration} if:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(T1)] $\Sig$ is a smooth closed hypersurface of $M$;
\item[(T2)] $G$ has corank exactly one at every point of $\Sig$, and the radical lines assemble into a smooth line subbundle $\shL \subset T^*M|_\Sig$, locally spanned by a covector field $k$;
\item[(T3)] (transversality) the degeneration is first-order: the canonical section $\delta$ of Lemma \claimref{sheaf.invariant-form-t3} below is nowhere vanishing on $\Sig$.
\end{enumerate}

sheaf.weights-are-forced-are-kasner-expo.formal:
For the Schwarzschild gravitational field $(\hb, \Omega)$ on flat spacetime (Definition \claimref{obj.synthesis.gtg-flat-spacetime-gauge-fields}), within the class of diagonal rescalings $r^{a_i}$ of the Schwarzschild coordinate coframe, demand that its derived cometric, read on the rescaled coframe, extend continuously to $E$. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item requiring a corank-one limit whose dying entry is the Killing-time entry, vanishing at first order, forces $(a_r, a_t, a_\theta) = (\tfrac12, 0, 1)$, uniquely: the degeneration-sheaf regime of Theorem \claimref{sheaf.schwarzschild-degeneration-boundary-tame};
\item requiring a nondegenerate limit forces $(a_r, a_t, a_\theta) = (\tfrac12, -\tfrac12, 1)$, uniquely: the exceptional regime of Proposition \claimref{sheaf.exceptional-algebra-black-hole}.
\end{enumerate}
The naming of the dying entry in (1) is not a free choice but is read off the gravitational field: the radical of the actual degeneration is the Killing-time covector (Theorem \claimref{sheaf.schwarzschild-degeneration-boundary-tame}(4)); without naming it, other slot assignments exist (for instance $(1, -\tfrac12, 1)$ sends the radial entry to zero at first order instead), so the forcing is per named profile. Converted to proper time $\tau_{\mathrm{p}} \propto r^{3/2}$, the exceptional weights are the Kasner exponents $(-\tfrac13, \tfrac23, \tfrac23)$ of Fact \claimref{sheaf.kasner-data-interior}, with $\sum p_i = \sum p_i^2 = 1$. This settles the Kasner-weights conjecture for Schwarzschild within the diagonal class: given the dying direction determined by the field, the weights of the anisotropic completion are not chosen, they are forced, and they are the Kasner exponents.

synthesis.imported-stiefel-connectivity-.formal:
Let $V_{k}(\R^{m})$ be the Stiefel manifold of orthonormal $k$-frames in $\R^{m}$ and $\mathrm{Gr}_{k}(\R^{m})$ the Grassmannian of $k$-planes, with tautological bundle $\gamma_{k}^{m}$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] \emph{(Connectivity.)} $V_{k}(\R^{m})$ is $(m - k - 1)$-connected: $\pi_{i}V_{k}(\R^{m}) = 0$ for $i \le m - k - 1$. Hence every map from a CW complex of dimension at most $m - k - 1$ into $V_{k}(\R^{m})$ is null-homotopic.
\item[(ii)] \emph{(Classification in a range.)} If $X$ is a CW complex of dimension less than $m - k$, the map $f \mapsto f^{*}\gamma_{k}^{m}$ is a bijection from homotopy classes of maps $X \to \mathrm{Gr}_{k}(\R^{m})$ onto isomorphism classes of real vector bundles of rank $k$ over $X$ (onto, not necessarily injective, when the dimension equals $m - k$). In particular two rank-$k$ subbundles of the trivial bundle $X \times \R^{m}$ that are isomorphic as bundles are homotopic through subbundles.
\end{enumerate}
\emph{Provenance:} \cite[Section 5]{MilnorStasheff1974}; \cite[Chapters 3 and 8]{Husemoller1994}; \cite[Section 4.2]{Hatcher2002}.

synthesis.transverse-corank-two-walls-ar.formal:
Let $M$ be a smooth manifold of dimension $n \geq 2$ with a smooth cometric $G$, and let $\Sig$ be the set where $G$ degenerates. In a local frame $G$ is a smooth map to the real symmetric $n \times n$ matrices; call $G$ \emph{transverse} at a point $x$ of corank $r$ if this map is transverse at $x$ to the submanifold of corank-$r$ matrices, which has codimension $r(r+1)/2$ (a frame-independent condition). Write $(p_0, q_0)$ for the signature of $G_x$ on a complement of its radical.
\begin{enumerate}
\item \emph{(Stable wild points.)} Let $n \geq 3$ and let $G$ be transverse at a point $x$ of corank two. Then $\det G$ vanishes to second order at $x$, near $x$ the set $\Sig$ is a quadric cone in three transverse coordinates times $\R^{n-3}$, and the radical at $x$ has rank two, so (T1) and (T2) of Hypotheses \claimref{sheaf.tameness} fail near $x$. Every cometric close to $G$ in $C^1$ near $x$ has a transverse corank-two point near $x$. Hence for every $n \geq 3$ the cometrics that are not tame contain a nonempty $C^1$-open set: tameness is not generic. The corank-two points of a transverse cometric form a submanifold of codimension three, worldlines when $n = 4$.
\item \emph{(Three signatures, one of them two-time.)} Every neighbourhood of such a point meets the three signatures $(p_0{+}2, q_0)$, $(p_0{+}1, q_0{+}1)$, $(p_0, q_0{+}2)$; if $(1,3)$ is one of them, $(2,2)$ is another.
\item \emph{(Surfaces.)} For $n = 2$, a cometric transverse at every point of $\Sig$ has no point of corank two and is tame.
\end{enumerate}
